tps://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/afghan-general-who-led-last-ditch-effort-to-stop-taliban-takeover-now-drives-uber-in-washington/articleshow/133654128.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/afghan-general-who-led-last-ditch-effort-to-stop-taliban-takeover-now-drives-uber-in-washington/articleshow/133654128.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T18:45:50+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133654252,imgsize-96915.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 1952, Rockefeller saw untouched St. John; his group later donated 5,000 acres for a US park]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133651302.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Laurance Rockefeller's 1952 boat trip inspired him to preserve St. John's undeveloped island beauty. His organization acquired nearly 5,000 acres, which became the park's foundation. Congress established Virgin Islands National Park in 1956, preserving tropical forests and beaches. Additional submerged lands were incorporated later, expanding the protected area significantly. This conservation effort ensures the island's natural landscapes remain protected for future generations.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/laurance-rockefeller-first-saw-undeveloped-st-john-from-a-boat-in-1952-his-conservation-group-later-acquired-about-5000-acres-and-gave-them-to-create-a-us-national-park/articleshow/133651302.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/laurance-rockefeller-first-saw-undeveloped-st-john-from-a-boat-in-1952-his-conservation-group-later-acquired-about-5000-acres-and-gave-them-to-create-a-us-national-park/articleshow/133651302.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T16:57:49+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133651462,imgsize-1294375.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Man spends life’s earnings on 130-acre gravel pit, turns it into wildlife haven]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133647216.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[The Cotswolds in England has about 170 lakes, most of them former gravel pits transformed after extraction ended. At Log House Holidays, 53 hectares of land were restored with 50,000 trees, wildflowers and reeds, creating habitats for birds and wildlife. The estate is now a designated Site of Special Scientific Interest and also hosts holiday cabins.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/a-man-spent-his-lifes-earnings-buying-a-130-acre-abandoned-gravel-pit-he-planted-50000-trees-and-years-later-the-barren-land-had-become-a-thriving-wildlife-oasis/articleshow/133647216.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/a-man-spent-his-lifes-earnings-buying-a-130-acre-abandoned-gravel-pit-he-planted-50000-trees-and-years-later-the-barren-land-had-become-a-thriving-wildlife-oasis/articleshow/133647216.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T17:10:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133647804,imgsize-156396.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Melbourne's M80 became Victoria's first major road with recycled content in every pavement layer]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133650050.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Melbourne's M80 Ring Road upgrade utilized recycled materials throughout its pavement layers. This project incorporated waste equivalent to millions of plastic bags and glass bottles. Recycled pavement materials and eMesh were also extensively used in the construction. The upgrade aimed to improve capacity, safety, and traffic reliability on the freeway. This initiative demonstrated a significant milestone in using recycled content for major infrastructure.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-2022-melbournes-m80-became-victorias-first-major-road-project-with-recycled-content-in-every-pavement-layer-reusing-35-5-million-plastic-bags-and-22-million-bottles/articleshow/133650050.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-2022-melbournes-m80-became-victorias-first-major-road-project-with-recycled-content-in-every-pavement-layer-reusing-35-5-million-plastic-bags-and-22-million-bottles/articleshow/133650050.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T17:10:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133650306,imgsize-2258897.cms"/></item></channel></rss>